# Copy to .env. Everything is optional: with no keys the swarm runs in simulation,
# and the claude-cli / codex-cli providers use whatever login those CLIs already have.

# Server
PORT=4317
SWARM_DB_PATH=./data/swarm.db
# Bind address. Keep 127.0.0.1 until the dashboard has authentication.
HOST=127.0.0.1

# API providers (production). Leave blank to use the CLI providers instead.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
OPENAI_API_KEY=
# DeepSeek has no default endpoint on purpose: founder dossiers are personal data. Set the base
# URL of a hosting provider you have approved for it (for example a US-hosted one).
DEEPSEEK_API_KEY=
DEEPSEEK_BASE_URL=
META_API_KEY=
META_BASE_URL=

# Leave these two unset unless a host spells the "answer fast" switch differently. A runner
# call that names no effort is sent {"thinking":{"type":"disabled"}} on DeepSeek and
# {"reasoning_effort":"minimal"} on Meta, because both reason by default and take tens of
# seconds to start answering if they do. Set to {} to send nothing extra.
# DEEPSEEK_FAST_PARAMS=
# META_FAST_PARAMS=

# Sources
GITHUB_TOKEN=
# How the hunt's page reader names itself to sites: a product name, a version, and a way to
# reach you, for example  my-fund-reader/1.0 (+https://example.com/reader). A site's
# robots.txt can then allow or refuse it by name. Unset, it is "web-reader/1.0".
# HUNT_USER_AGENT=
# Optional proxy for every read, as an http:// proxy URL, for example a Bright Data datacenter
# endpoint: http://brd-customer-<id>-zone-<zone>:<password>@brd.superproxy.io:22225 . Account
# vendors require an exclusive IPv4 proxy per account and forbid residential or ISP proxies for
# logins, so use a dedicated datacenter IP, not the residential network. LinkedIn reads use it
# unless the account supplies its own proxy below.
# HUNT_PROXY_URL=
# Logged-in LinkedIn sessions, one Cookie header value per line (copy the Cookie request
# header from a browser signed in to the account). The hunt's reader and the quote checker
# send one to linkedin.com only and rotate through the list. Each account keeps a human
# rhythm on its own (a workday with lunch, bursts, a daily cap, lighter weekends), seeded
# from its cookie, so there is nothing to configure; reads outside the rhythm are held back,
# and a LinkedIn refusal rests the account until its next workday. Run the server in the
# partner's timezone so the workdays are the right hours. Sessions you create or buy are
# added the same way.
# LINKEDIN_COOKIES=
# Optional lines aligned to LINKEDIN_COOKIES: the sticky proxy and the user agent that belong
# to that account, so one account never jumps IP or browser and is not banned for it.
# LINKEDIN_PROXIES=
# LINKEDIN_USER_AGENTS=
# Read LinkedIn through the Octo Browser instead of plain requests: one profile per account,
# a real Chromium window with its own fingerprint and proxy, driven like a person (one tab,
# a dwell between pages, scrolling as a reader does). Leave blank to read with requests.
# When set, there must be one UUID per LINKEDIN_COOKIES line, aligned by index; create
# the profiles in the Octo app on this machine (each with its own sticky proxy) and copy
# their UUIDs. The Octo app must be running and signed in; the API is local, no key
# needed. Run only one swarm process against the same profiles: two would stop and start
# each other's browser windows.
# OCTO_PROFILE_UUIDS=
# OCTO_LOCAL_URL=http://localhost:58888
# Professional-profile data from a vendor under contract (People Data Labs). Leave blank to run
# without it. Each record the vendor returns costs a credit, outside the run's own spend cap, so
# there is a separate ceiling on records per day (default 50).
PDL_API_KEY=
PDL_RECORDS_PER_DAY=50
# Bright Data's LinkedIn Scraper API, for a known profile's about, experience and education and
# for a name search. Preferred for lookups when its key is set; People Data Labs remains the
# discovery feed. Billed per delivered record (5,000 free per month), also outside the run's
# spend cap, with its own daily ceiling. The dataset id is optional.
# BRIGHTDATA_API_KEY=
# BRIGHTDATA_RECORDS_PER_DAY=50
# BRIGHTDATA_LINKEDIN_DATASET_ID=

# The model that judges past companies in the past-founders eval, as provider:model or
# provider:model:effort. Unset, the analysts' own model is used, which costs far more.
# EVAL_JUDGE=claude-cli:claude-haiku-5-5
# Where datasets built from real past data are kept. Default: evals/datasets
# EVAL_DATASETS_DIR=
